%% Lifecycle of the file share OBJECT itself - the states between live and permanently gone, and the deliberate path an attacker uses to skip them
%% Source: codex/runbook-restore-file-share-and-share-soft-delete.md class 4; Microsoft Learn "Azure file share soft delete" (1 to 365 days, default 7, undelete restores snapshots, permanent-delete procedure), "About Azure Files backup" (infinite lease, Backup enables soft delete at 14 days), "Recover a deleted storage account" (14 days, private endpoints not recreated) (read 2026-09-03)
stateDiagram-v2
  direction TB

  state "Storage account stmtyfiles01 - Microsoft.Storage provider" as ACCOUNT {
    direction TB

    state "Live - the share is mounted, synced and being backed up" as LIVE
    state "Live and leased - Azure Backup holds an infinite lease on every snapshot it took" as LEASED
    state "Soft deleted - listed only with --include-deleted, carries a deletedTime, a deletedShareVersion and remainingRetentionDays" as SOFT
    state "Expired - the retention clock ran out and the share is unrecoverable" as GONE
    state "Soft delete turned OFF at the file service level - new deletions are immediate and permanent" as OFFSTATE

    [*] --> LIVE : az storage share-rm create
    LIVE --> LEASED : Enable-AzRecoveryServicesBackupProtection, then the first snapshot
    LEASED --> LEASED : every scheduled backup adds another leased snapshot, up to 200

    LEASED --> SOFT : share deleted - the lease makes this several steps rather than one click, it does not forbid it
    LIVE --> SOFT : share deleted while soft delete is enabled on the account
    SOFT --> LEASED : undelete - the share AND every snapshot on it return exactly as they were
    SOFT --> GONE : remainingRetentionDays reaches zero

    LIVE --> OFFSTATE : Update-AzStorageFileServiceProperty -EnableShareDeleteRetentionPolicy false
    OFFSTATE --> GONE : delete - nothing catches it
    OFFSTATE --> LIVE : soft delete re-enabled, which does not help a share already gone
  }

  state "Account-level events that take the share with them" as OUTER {
    direction TB
    state "Storage account deleted - every share, snapshot and private endpoint goes at once" as ACCTDEL
    state "Account restored - best effort, portal Restore deleted account" as ACCTBACK
    state "Account unrecoverable - name reused, resource group deleted, or past 14 days" as ACCTGONE

    ACCTDEL --> ACCTBACK : within 14 days, ARM account, resource group still present, name not taken
    ACCTDEL --> ACCTGONE : any of those four conditions fails
  }

  LEASED --> ACCTDEL : account-level deletion ignores share soft delete entirely
  ACCTBACK --> LIVE : the shares come back, but the file private endpoint does NOT
  ACCTGONE --> VAULTONLY
  GONE --> VAULTONLY

  state "Only the vault-standard copy is left - restore to an ALTERNATE share in a new account, then rebuild the cloud endpoint" as VAULTONLY

  note right of ACCOUNT
    The bypass to state out loud in the report.
    Permanently deleting a soft-deleted share is a documented
    three-step procedure - undelete it, disable soft delete on
    the file service, delete it again. Every step is a data-plane
    or account-property action that Azure Backup and the MUA
    Resource Guard never see. Only RBAC on the storage account,
    the CanNotDelete lock and Azure Policy stand between a
    compromised Storage Account Contributor and a permanent loss.
  end note

  note right of OUTER
    Contoso defaults that matter here.
    Soft delete is an ACCOUNT-level setting covering every share
    on stmtyfiles01, configurable from 1 to 365 days with a default
    of 7. Azure Backup raises it to 14 when protection is enabled,
    and Contoso baseline holds it at 14 or more. Recovering a
    deleted ACCOUNT does not recreate the private endpoint, its DNS
    zone group or the Infoblox forwarder record - plan that rebuild
    into the runbook, or sync stays broken after a successful restore.
  end note
